# TernarySymplecticArrays.jl

TernarySymplecticArrays.jl is a Julia package for bounded, allocation-free linear algebra over the balanced ternary alphabet

\[
\mathcal{T} = \{-1, 0, +1\}.
\]

The package is designed around fixed-size discrete data, low-allocation arithmetic, and explicit invariant checks for constrained transformations. It is intentionally scoped as a numerical safety layer rather than a general-purpose continuum symplectic engine.

## Mathematical scope

This repository is intentionally conservative about its geometric claims.

- The discrete element set is the balanced ternary alphabet \(\{-1, 0, +1\}\).
- A canonical symplectic form is represented by the standard block matrix
  \[
  J = \begin{bmatrix} 0 & I \\ -I & 0 \end{bmatrix}.
  \]
- A real matrix \(\Psi\) is symplectic precisely when it satisfies the invariant
  \[
  \Psi^T J \Psi = J.
  \]
- The scalar condition \(\det(\Psi) = \pm 1\) is a useful sanity check, but it is not the defining condition for symplecticity.
- This package does not claim a general Lie-theoretic symplectic solver or a full continuum symplectic algebra implementation; it focuses on deterministic discrete map-preservation checks and numerical invariants for a bounded array layer.

## What the package does

- Defines a balanced ternary scalar type and fixed-size static array containers.
- Provides zero-allocation arithmetic patterns for compact fixed-size vectors.
- Implements base-3 packing and unpacking helpers for ternary triplets.
- Validates matrix structure and transformation invariants under a chosen symplectic form.
- Keeps determinant checks as secondary consistency checks rather than the primary definition of validity.

## Repository layout

- `src/Types.jl` — ternary scalar and static-array definitions
- `src/Operators.jl` — arithmetic and packing operators
- `src/Pullbacks.jl` — symplectic-form preservation checks
- `src/Bindings.jl` — FFI-oriented serialization boundary
- `proofs/Lean/` — Lean 4 algebraic validation fragments
- `tests/RunTests.jl` — package validation harness

## Installation

From the repository root:

```bash
julia --project=. -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.instantiate()'
```

Then run the test suite:

```bash
julia --project=. tests/RunTests.jl
```

